      "worked": "Since $5>4$, use the third branch.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(5)&=5b+2\\\\5b+2&=12\\\\b&=2\\end{align*}$$\n\nAt $x=4$, using $b=2$:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(4)&=4a+2\\\\f_L(4)&=4a+2\\\\f_R(4)&=10\\end{align*}$$\n\nContinuity requires all three values to agree.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}4a+2&=10\\\\a&=2\\end{align*}$$\n\nAt $x=1$, using $a=2$:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(1)&=4\\\\f_L(1)&=4c\\\\f_R(1)&=4\\end{align*}$$\n\nContinuity again requires all three values to agree.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}4c&=4\\\\c&=1\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus $a=2$, $b=2$ and $c=1$. Each branch is a polynomial, and the branches agree at both boundaries, so $f$ is continuous throughout its domain.",
